---
import PostLayout from "../../../components/PostLayout.astro";

export const meta = {
    title: "All my download metrics in one place",
    date: new Date(2026, 0, 2),
    description:
        "Trying to find a single view of package download stats across registries",
};
---

<PostLayout meta={meta}>
    <p>
        I've been working with companies that have multiple libraries in
        different languages that get published to a bunch of repositories.
        At Cryptlex, I developed packages for 4 platforms out of the 12 or so
        that are supported. These get shipped to Crates.io, NuGet, npmjs, and
        Maven Central repositories.
    </p><p>
        I wanted to understand how these packages are adopted and see a
        normalized view of this data so I could prioritize which of these to
        focus on based on adoption. As my projects often go, I wanted this to
        be a frontend only project.
    </p><p>After looking at the repositories, I found</p><ol>
        <li>
            <a href="https://docs.pypi.org/api/stats/">PyPI</a> only provides
            stats for the top 100 packages on the repository. Alternatively, <a
                href="https://pypistats.org/api/">pypistats</a
            > has download data and provides a JSON API for the same. The BigQuery
            tables are also open to be downloaded/queried.
        </li><li>
            The <a href="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json">NuGet API</a>
            provides some statistics but the a) the CORS headers make it
            unfavorable for the application to be frontend only, and b) the <a
                href="https://www.nuget.org/stats/packages/Cryptlex.LexActivator?groupby=Version"
                >data</a
            > is only available for the past 6 weeks.
        </li><li>
            The npmjs registry endpoint (<code
                >https://registry.npmjs.org/<em>package-name</em></code
            >) was the most permissive out of the lot. No CORS, which is why
            it powers frontend applications like <a
                href="https://www.npmcharts.com/">npmcharts</a
            >.
        </li>
    </ol><p>
        Before I could get to crates.io or maven central, I found <a
            href="https://libraries.io/">libraries.io</a
        >, which seems to do exactly what I'm aiming to do here. I have yet
        to use it, I will write more when I do.
    </p>
</PostLayout>
